WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.930
IVAN NARDINI: Hi, everyone, and
welcome to the Google IO 2022.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.130
I'm Ivan Nardini.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.320
I'm a Customer Engineer
at Google Cloud,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.230
and today I want to
tell you a story.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:19.530
That story begins with you, who
just joined the data science

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.470
team of an important
banking company.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:26.280
Congratulations, now your team
is part of the data office,

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.980
and is responsible for
building innovative solutions

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.710
across several business units.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:35.370
On day one Maya, the product
manager of the Fraud Detection

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.250
System, has a meeting
with you and your team.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.770
She shows a rules-driven
engine composed

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:42.900
of a set of if/then,
else statements,

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.560
and logic are coded by
SMEs in collaboration

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.100
with the software
engineering team.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.620
For each transaction,
she said that if it

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:54.350
comes from the country
X, and the cardholder

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.600
has several requests
within the last hour

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.210
with a certain amount,
then the system must

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:02.870
assign a fraud score of 0.95.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.060
With that, Maya
had some concerns

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:08.590
about the main challenges
of the actual system.

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:12.190
First of all, human rules
are biased by definition.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:14.230
They represent a
subjective component,

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:18.280
since different experts design
rules in different ways.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.940
Secondly, the system is
hard to scale because rules

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:23.800
are required to be hard-coded.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.370
Each time a new fraud pattern
is discovered by investigators,

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.560
the team needs to
manually express it

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:33.170
as if/then, else statements,
and integrate them.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:36.490
Last but not least, it
also is hard to contain.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:39.610
Indeed, we ended up
with [INAUDIBLE] rules,

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:42.520
each of them for
each fraud scenario.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.160
And its maintainers are
investigators and software

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.830
engineers that tend
to come and go,

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:49.870
bringing with them
information about how

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:51.800
rules make the system.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.100
So because of those
challenges, Maya

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:57.220
expressed the needs of
building a new system.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:00.520
She wants to implement a modern
data-driven fraud detection

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:01.390
engine.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:03.280
And because the company
is going to have

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.320
a process of digital
transformation,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:09.130
she asked you and your team
to design it on Google Cloud.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.080
She wants a machine
learning model

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:14.620
to estimate the probability of
each transaction to be a fraud.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.350
She expects that the
model does not require

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:19.510
any manual intervention.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:22.660
And it needs to be
interpretable at the same time

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:26.320
in order to allow her SME
and investigators to validate

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.740
its results and, if
necessary, improve it.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.020
As I said, you are
part of the team that

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.830
has been assigned
to design and build

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:35.950
the system on Google Cloud.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:40.950
So my question is, where
would you start from?

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:42.930
Well, I'm a customer engineer.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.000
That's my job, and
I spend my time

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.550
talking with data science teams.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:51.120
And I notice that the model
and its associated technology

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.310
tend to be seen
as a shopping list

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:56.130
without considering the
impact of their requirements

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.650
and dependencies on
the entire system.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:02.010
Also, it happens sometimes
that phases like the model

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.490
experimentation tend to drive
choices about its components

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.300
and how they should work.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.440
But the thing is that
building machine learning

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.850
models is just one phase, and
it has nothing automagical.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:17.800
If you don't feed it
with good quality data,

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.170
it will not generate
good insights.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:21.870
And if you don't
make it modular,

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:26.460
you will not able to adapt it
when new patterns of data come.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:29.370
And this is just to say
that the model is important,

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.790
but sometimes the big
picture is missing.

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:35.190
And when you need to
production analyze your model,

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:37.290
when you need to make
it a product that

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:42.300
will generate business value,
well, the context matters most.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:46.030
To build it, you need
culture and best practices.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:49.520
That's why MLOps came out.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.300
Indeed, the concept
itself expressed

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.020
the set of culture,
practice, and technology that

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:57.070
aim at unifying ML
development with operations

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.620
in order to put a
model into production.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:02.890
At Google, we were able to
turn this concept concretely.

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:06.460
For example, one of the largest
production ML systems at Google

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:09.610
has thousands of ML models
training concurrently,

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:12.310
continuously retrained
with new data every hour,

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:15.970
and continuously deploying the
global serving infrastructure.

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:20.200
So we really know what MLOps
means, and as Google Cloud,

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:23.890
we want to share those expertise
and technology with you.

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:26.560
About the expertise,
you have people like me

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.040
that are ready to support you
in your ML journey day by day.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:32.260
But talking about
technology, that's

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:35.590
the reason why we launched
Vertex AI, which is the Google

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:38.500
Cloud managed machine
learning platform

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.690
to help you accelerate
experimentation,

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:44.030
and deployment of
ML models at scale.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:47.150
All you need to know is that the
platform has several building

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:50.150
blocks that allow you to
cover the entire lifecycle,

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:52.400
from data ingestion
to model training

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.010
all the way down to model
prediction and model

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:58.250
monitoring, in order to
production analyze your models.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:00.770
So now that you have the
technology, the next question

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:04.160
is, what would be building
the new fraud detection

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:05.480
system on Vertex AI?

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:07.230
What will it look like?

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.430
How will it be better
compared to what you have?

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:13.340
Well, I already know the
answer to those questions.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:13.910
Why?

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:15.790
Because I did it.

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:18.910
In collaboration with some of
my colleagues at Google Cloud,

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:21.430
we built Fraud Finder.

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:25.930
Fraud Finder is a project that
comes from some customer needs

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:28.810
to apply the state of the art
of data science and machine

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:31.840
learning to detect fraudulent
transactions at scale

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:33.100
in real time.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.560
It's still a work in
progress, but me and the team

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.420
learned so many things along
the road that we thought,

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:39.950
why don't we share
our experience?

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:41.690
So here I am.

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:43.310
Are you with me?

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:45.150
Let's go on.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.470
To build a simple
fraud detection system,

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:49.470
as Fraud Finder,
you start from data

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:51.180
of historical transactions.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:54.600
Just to clarify in your
case, a transaction

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.300
is an event recorded
at 5:00 AM today,

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:00.180
associated with a customer
with a particular ID, who

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.110
made a payment of
a certain amount

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:04.080
to an identified merchant.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.250
Now, part of that information
cannot be consumed either

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.380
by a model as they are.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.350
In fact, the model needs
numbers, and only some of them

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:15.290
are numericals.

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:18.680
Also, you can think of applying
several data transformation

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:21.020
techniques to derive
new variables which

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:24.830
will be relevant to predict if
a transaction is a fraud or not.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:29.120
Will the number of transactions
customers made recently matter?

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.410
What about the average
transaction amount

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:32.930
in the last week?

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:36.080
Could the terminal be
compromised for any customer

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:37.670
who is using it?

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:40.850
In other terms, you
define a set of features

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:43.250
to identify customer
spending behavior,

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:45.560
and characterize
the risk of fraud

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:49.580
associated with the terminals
over different time windows.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:52.610
And you can do that
by using BigQuery,

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:58.100
which allows you to analyze
terabytes of data per second.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:00.050
At that time, you
get your features

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:01.940
and you are ready
to train your model.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:05.430
But let's consider some
aspects for a second.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:08.520
First of all, in a real
time fraud detection system,

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:12.170
some of these features cannot be
calculated on the fly when you

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:13.970
serve the model.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:15.950
How will you get
the average amount

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:19.280
of transactions over the last
three days in milliseconds?

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.160
You need a way to pass
them as inputs to the model

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.770
if you want to generate
online predictions.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:28.940
Now, BigQuery is not engineered
for low latency reads.

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:30.950
That's why you need to
introduce and optimize

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:33.200
a data store for
lookup operations

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:35.390
at scale in low latency.

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:38.000
But there is another aspect
that needs to be highlighted.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.910
In that real time system,
you have a high volume

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:43.220
of transactions you
can use to calculate

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:46.340
those features we keep floating
and collecting constantly

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:47.720
over time.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:50.330
It implies that you'll
always get fresh data

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:51.770
to reconstruct features.

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:53.900
And depending on when
you decide to calculate

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:56.210
one feature or another,
you can end it up

00:07:56.210 --> 00:08:00.000
with a set of features that
are not aligned in time.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.420
For example, you
can keep calculating

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:04.880
the number of transactions
over the last three days.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:07.400
Each day is at midnight,
but at the same time,

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:10.520
you will need to calculate the
average amount of transactions

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:14.780
over the last three hours
along the day several times.

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:17.630
And as you can imagine,
this has implications when

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:20.070
you need to train your model.

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:22.710
Indeed, when labels
become available,

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:26.490
those features will not be
aligned with them as well.

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:30.000
In your case, you will know if
a transaction is a fraud or not

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.380
only after some time.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:35.010
The investigation is run and
validated your predictions.

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:37.470
Because of that, it
sounds incredibly

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:39.960
difficult to say
which set of features

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:42.720
contain the most up-to-date
historical information

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:45.930
associated with the label
you want to predict.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:48.450
And when you are not
able to guarantee that,

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:50.970
the performance of your model
would be badly affected.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:53.430
Because you serve
representative features

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:56.490
of the data and the label from
the field when the model goes

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:57.850
live.

00:08:57.850 --> 00:09:01.440
So you need a way to get the
most up-to-date features,

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:05.100
you calculate over time before
the labels become available.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:07.830
In other terms, you need
a time travel machine.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:11.010
For your features, we
respect your labels.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:13.020
For all those reasons
I just mentioned,

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:15.960
you may decide to introduce
a Feature Store, which

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:18.990
provides you a service that
help you to address data

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:22.170
leakage by providing
point-in-time lookups which

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:24.750
fetch the most up-to-date
features with respect

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:27.150
of the time labels
become availables.

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:29.820
And at the same time,
it serves those features

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:32.490
at scale with low latency,
as they were available

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:36.030
at training time, mitigating
the way possible training

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:37.650
and service queue.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:40.620
So once you ingest the features
into the Feature Store,

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:43.320
you are ready to train
your model offline.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:45.960
You fetch a sample of them,
aligned with the label,

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:48.180
into a notebook environment.

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:50.670
You use this sample
for running experiments

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:52.890
by defining different
training configurations

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:55.410
and tracking model
evaluation metrics.

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:57.600
And once you find the
best modeling approach,

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:01.530
you formalize the model training
as a pipeline component.

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:05.130
Then, you easily integrate that
component in machine learning

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:07.230
pipelines that
allow you to cover

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:09.630
all the steps required
to reproduce the model

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:10.990
training at scale.

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:14.580
And if, only if, the model
achieves certain threshold

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:17.820
levels in performance
metrics, then it

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:20.310
goes deploying to
serving environment,

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:22.380
for generating online
prediction when

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:25.040
a new request will require.

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:28.550
Notice how the Feature Store
passes the same aggregated

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:31.880
features you have calculated
offline to train the model

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:33.680
to produce online predictions.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:36.320
In other terms, it solved
the training service

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:38.480
queue I mentioned so far.

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.000
All I showed you until
now, from Feature Store

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:44.150
to model training with
pipelines and the model serving,

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:48.320
can be implemented on Vertex
AI, which provides you

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:50.180
all those capabilities
by letting

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:53.000
you keep focusing on
your code and improving

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.220
the quality of your solution.

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:59.180
And talking about the solution,
let me show you what we got.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:05.880
This is Fraud Finder, the
data application we worked on.

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:09.000
Once you pass some parameters
such as the Feature

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:11.820
Store, resource name,
and the endpoint ID,

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:14.130
you can start streaming
the transactions that

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:16.470
are generated for a
data synthesizer we

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:18.630
build in the backend.

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:21.700
After a while, if you
go in the Predict view,

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:24.210
you can see in yellow
the transactions that

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:26.460
were classified as a
fraud, with respect

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:28.810
to a particular
probability threshold.

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:31.560
And if you change it,
you can verify that fraud

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:33.960
becomes less frequent.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:36.510
Lastly, under the
Dashboard view,

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:39.210
you have the Profile
of Latency, which

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:41.850
is represented by the
distribution of prediction

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:44.700
requests over a
fraction of a second.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:47.370
As you can see, the back
end is doing a good job

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.830
if you consider that,
for each request,

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:53.310
it reads features from the
Vertex AI Feature Store.

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:55.890
It passes them into
Vertex AI endpoint

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:59.550
with transaction amount and
other variables from the field.

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:01.440
And the model
generates predictions

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:02.910
and return them back.

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:06.030
All this, thanks to Vertex AI.

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:08.520
About the rest, you also
have the distribution plot

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:11.130
of the fraudulent and
non-fraudulent transactions,

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:13.560
and you also have
some views showing

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:17.530
the active endpoints and the
model we have on Vertex AI.

00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:20.110
OK, this was just
an example of what

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:24.990
you can achieve by leveraging
Vertex AI for MLOps.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:26.670
We are at the end
of this journey.

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:29.040
To conclude, you
start from a system

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:32.910
that has biased because rules
were written by SMEs based

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:33.990
on their knowledge.

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:35.940
It was hard to
scale because rules

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:37.440
required to be hard coded.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:41.280
It was hard to maintain because
of its all rules it generates.

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:45.720
And today, I showed you how
you can solve those challenges

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:49.350
with a simple fraud detection
system, which leverages

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:51.810
both data to train
machine learning models,

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:54.970
and Vertex AI platform to
production analyze them.

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:58.980
So now that you know that this
is possible, it's your turn.

